### Bug description

I'm using `fast-check@4.6.0`. 

I was trying out `entityGraph` and came across an issue with `initialPoolConstraints`. If the field is just an empty object, the code below compiles. But once I start indicating the initial constraints for each entity, the code stops compiling in the following two ways.

First, the object of arbitraries thinks the object doesn't have fields in it. For example, I get this error:
```
Object literal may only specify known properties, but 'ref1Thing' does not exist in type 'Arbitraries<{ thing: unknown; ref0Or1Thing: unknown; ref0ToManyThings: unknown; bidirectionalReference: unknown; }>'. Did you mean to write 'ref0Or1Thing'?
```

Second, the functions in `unicityConstraints` no longer correctly infer what the `value` arg is for each node's function. They are always `unknown`. For example:
```typescript
{
  unicityConstraints: {
    // TSC: 'value' is of type 'unknown'.
    thing: (value) => value.name,
}
```

See Example 1 for the issue. 

I found that I could get the code to compile if I moved the args around and added types. See Example 2.

So, I tried to specify the type parameters around `entityGraph` and found that I can't reference the `EntityRelations` type. So, I tried to inline its definition but then found that I can't reference the `Relationship` type.

Looking through the codebase, it appears there's not a test case for this particular usage.

### To reproduce

Example 1

```typescript
import fc from 'fast-check';

const example = fc.entityGraph(
  {
    thing: { name: fc.stringMatching(/^[A-Z][a-z]*$/) },
    ref1Thing: { name: fc.stringMatching(/^[A-Z][a-z]*$/) },
    ref0Or1Thing: { name: fc.stringMatching(/^[A-Z][a-z]*$/) },
    ref0ToManyThings: { name: fc.stringMatching(/^[A-Z][a-z]*$/) },
    bidirectionalReference: { name: fc.stringMatching(/^[A-Z][a-z]*$/) },
  },
  {
    thing: {
      referencers: {
        arity: 'inverse',
        type: 'bidirectionalReference',
        forwardRelationship: 'thingForBidiRef',
      },
    },
    ref1Thing: {
      thingForRef1: { arity: '1', type: 'thing' },
    },
    ref0Or1Thing: {
      thingForRef2: { arity: '0-1', type: 'thing' },
    },
    ref0ToManyThings: {
      thingsForRef3: { arity: 'many', type: 'thing' },
    },
    bidirectionalReference: {
      thingForBidiRef: { arity: '1', type: 'thing' },
    },
  },
  {
    initialPoolConstraints: {
      // leaving any one of these fields uncommented prevents code from compiling
      // thing: undefined,
      // ref1Thing: undefined,
      // ref0Or1Thing: undefined,
      // ref0ToManyThings: undefined,
      // bidirectionalReference: undefined,
    },
    unicityConstraints: {
      thing: (value) => value.name,
      ref1Thing: (value) => value.name,
      ref0Or1Thing: (value) => value.name,
      ref0ToManyThings: (value) => value.name,
      bidirectionalReference: (value) => value.name,
    },
    noNullPrototype: true,
  },
);
```

Example 2
```typescript
import fc, { EntityGraphContraints } from 'fast-check';

const arbs = {
  thing: { name: fc.stringMatching(/^[A-Z][a-z]*$/) },
  ref1Thing: { name: fc.stringMatching(/^[A-Z][a-z]*$/) },
  ref0Or1Thing: { name: fc.stringMatching(/^[A-Z][a-z]*$/) },
  ref0ToManyThings: { name: fc.stringMatching(/^[A-Z][a-z]*$/) },
  bidirectionalReference: { name: fc.stringMatching(/^[A-Z][a-z]*$/) },
};

const relationships = {
  thing: {
    referencers: {
      arity: 'inverse' as const,
      type: 'bidirectionalReference' as const,
      forwardRelationship: 'thingForBidiRef' as const,
    },
  },
  ref1Thing: {
    thingForRef1: { arity: '1' as const, type: 'thing' as const },
  },
  ref0Or1Thing: {
    thingForRef2: { arity: '0-1' as const, type: 'thing' as const },
  },
  ref0ToManyThings: {
    thingsForRef3: { arity: 'many' as const, type: 'thing' as const },
  },
  bidirectionalReference: {
    thingForBidiRef: { arity: '1' as const, type: 'thing' as const },
  },
};

// Adding `EntityGraphContraints` here fixes the type inference issue
const constraints: EntityGraphContraints<{
  thing: { name: string };
  ref1Thing: { name: string };
  ref0Or1Thing: { name: string };
  ref0ToManyThings: { name: string };
  bidirectionalReference: { name: string };
}> = {
  initialPoolConstraints: {
    thing: undefined,
    ref1Thing: undefined,
    ref0Or1Thing: undefined,
    ref0ToManyThings: undefined,
    bidirectionalReference: undefined,
  },
  unicityConstraints: {
    thing: (value) => value.name,
    ref1Thing: (value) => value.name,
    ref0Or1Thing: (value) => value.name,
    ref0ToManyThings: (value) => value.name,
    bidirectionalReference: (value) => value.name,
  },
  noNullPrototype: true,
};

// this compiles
const example = fc.entityGraph(arbs, relationships, constraints);
```

### Expected behavior

I'd expect `entityGraph` to properly infer things.

